[{:seon.task/name "a-repeated-defect-writes-nothing"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by session STOREDIG, 2026-10-21 (why the standing root's 1 GiB LMDB store, `runs/store-full-20261021`, hit `:seon.config.store/lmdb-map-bytes` (1,073,741,824) under a mostly idle root). Measured on a clone of the store: 10 branches, nine of them with 407-411 datoms and 3 commits; main `run-20261021T144308-dd0ab3bc` (booted 14:43:08) has 455 datoms and 41,714 commits. Its recorded error (entity 142, signature 7fb6b004fbd91375, operation `seon.task/entity-value`, contract-error invalid-output) has `:seon.error/count` 41,709 with first-ms 14:45:54 and last-ms 14:47:04: 592 commits per second for 70.5 s, 15 MB/s, until the map was full. The store holds 250,438 konserve keys (all content-addressed index nodes) = 647.6 MB of values, 1,073,725,440 bytes of file: 6.0 nodes, 15.5 KB of values and 25.7 KB of file per commit. The head of that branch is ~70 nodes / 97 KB live: about 99.99% of the file is nodes no head reaches (nothing is collected; storage reclaim, DECISIONS 2026-10-20, is the separate L1 mechanism). Reproduced on a fresh store: one count-update commit costs 6.0 keys, 16.8 KB of values and 28 KB of file with the 4.5 KB `:seon.error/example` string present, 6.0 keys / 7.8 KB / 13 KB without it (0.8 ms per commit either way). The cause is a feedback loop closed by the error writer, not the work: (1) `seon.task/root` answered nil because the root entity had only `:seon.root/quiescent` (the boot fact was refused, fixed since by `seon.db/install-missing!`, task seon.db/a-kept-store-gains-new-attributes-at-open), so `seon.task/entity-value` returned `[:root nil]`, which its output contract refuses; (2) that throw is in `seon.reconcile/reconciler-proc`'s `:main` step, and a Flow step that throws leaves the proc's state unchanged (docs/flow.md, 'A throw in transform'), so its `:t` stays behind and the next commit takes the relist branch (`seon.reconcile/index`), which throws again; (3) root's api folds each thrown error (`seon.error/fold`) and `seon.root/flush-problems` writes it to main as one transaction whenever none is in flight, with `:seon.error/count` and `:seon.error/last-ms` changed by `seon.error/flush-tx`; (4) that commit is the reconciler's next input: go to 2. The 'window' of `seon.error/flush-tx` is one write round trip, so a storm costs one commit per round trip, unbounded in rate and size. The first cause is fixed; the loop remains for any defect a main observer throws on every commit. Correct (AGENTS.md section 2: if nothing has changed, we do nothing; a defect holds its message per key until the code is fixed): a signature already on main is not written again for one more occurrence. `seon.error/flush-tx` writes a signature when it is first seen by the observer and not already on main, and writes nothing for repeats; the repeat count lives in the observer's fold (a value, `seon.error/fold`), `seon.problems/report` and `my.root/problems` read the live fold beside main's records so the count shown is the true one, and the fold's count is made durable by a write root makes anyway (the first record of the next signature, or root's stop), never by a timer. This retires the assertion of `seon.problems-test/a-storm-is-one-write-per-window` that counts are absolute across windows in the stored fact (its three-window block changes to read the fold), and deletes `:dirty?` for a written signature. Root's gate before approving: which mechanism does this (ours: the fold already holds the count), what v1 did (CLAUDE.md of v1: 'records Seon's own errors once per signature at main, with a count... repeats only count'), the field's way (Kubernetes event aggregation counts in memory, patches at a bounded rate), no new format or key. Rejected: advancing the reconciler's `:t` on a failed step (hides this loop in one proc; any observer of main that fails per commit would loop the same way) and throttling the writer (a clock decides). Separate finding, not fixed here: `seon.error/flush-tx` stores the first example as an unbounded `pr-str` (`*print-length*` nil), 4.5 KB here, which is 2 of the 6 nodes (4-6 KB each) rewritten per commit; stored text should be the value printer's printed value, within its budget (AGENTS.md section 3 'one printer'; DECISIONS 'the printer's budget bounds stored text'). Proofs: `seon.problems-test/a-written-signature-is-not-written-again` (flush-tx: 1,000 more occurrences of a written signature produce no tx) and `seon.problems-test/a-defect-in-the-reconciler-does-not-feed-itself` (root's path in miniature: ten rounds of a throwing reconciler step, fold, flush and commit write at most one commit to main). Done (session ERRLOOP): entries carry :base once written; a repeat sets :pending? (not :dirty?); `seon.error/flush-tx` writes :dirty? entries and, when it writes any, the pending counts with them; `seon.root/flush-problems` sends nothing for an empty tx; `seon.problems/report` takes the fold (`seon.root/errors`) for true counts. Not done: the pending counts are not flushed at root's stop (the writer's port closes in the step that would write them). Estimate: an idle system writes 0 commits and 0 bytes per repeat; the first record is one commit of ~6 nodes (~13-28 KB)."
